# C# and Godot 4.5 Patterns

## Core C# Requirements

### Class Declaration
```csharp
using Godot;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public partial class EnemyController : Node2D
{
    // Implementation
}
```

**CRITICAL**: All C# classes must use `partial` keyword for Godot 4.5 compatibility.

### Method Signatures
```csharp
// ✅ CORRECT: Override virtual methods with proper signatures
public override void _Ready()
{
    // Initialization
}

public override void _Process(double delta)
{
    // Game logic
}

public override void _PhysicsProcess(double delta)
{
    // Physics logic
}

public override void _Input(InputEvent @event)
{
    // Input handling
}

// ❌ WRONG: Missing override or wrong signature
public void _ready() // Wrong casing, missing override
{
}

public override void _Process(float delta) // Wrong type - should be double
{
}
```

### Signal Declarations and Usage
```csharp
// ✅ CORRECT: Signal declaration
[Signal]
public delegate void HealthChangedEventHandler(int newHealth, int oldHealth);

[Signal]
public delegate void DefeatedEventHandler(int enemyId, Vector2 position, int scoreValue);

// ✅ CORRECT: Signal emission
public void TakeDamage(int amount)
{
    int oldHealth = _health;
    _health -= amount;
    EmitSignal(SignalName.HealthChanged, _health, oldHealth);
}

// ✅ CORRECT: Signal connection
public override void _Ready()
{
    HealthChanged += OnHealthChanged;
    EventBus.EnemyDefeated += OnEnemyDefeated;
}

// ✅ CORRECT: Signal disconnection
public override void _ExitTree()
{
    HealthChanged -= OnHealthChanged;
    EventBus.EnemyDefeated -= OnEnemyDefeated;
}

// ❌ WRONG: String-based signal names
EmitSignal("health_changed", _health, oldHealth); // Use SignalName enum instead
```

